Q: Angular pipe not reacting to observable/signal? A: <p>Technically speaking, you can pretty much already do this by returning a computed in the transform function</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>const multiplier = signal(1);

@Pipe({
  name: 'multi',
})
export class Multi {

  transform(value: number) {
    return computed(() =&gt; value * multiplier());
  }
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [Multi],
  template: `
    @if (enabled()) {

      @let m = value() | multi;

      &lt;p&gt;{{ m() }}&lt;p&gt;
      &lt;button (click)=&quot;value.set(value() + 1)&quot;&gt;Increase value&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button (click)=&quot;multiplier.set(multiplier() + 1)&quot;&gt;Increase multiplier&lt;/button&gt;
    }
    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;button (click)=&quot;enabled.set(!enabled())&quot;&gt;Toggle enabled&lt;/button&gt;
  `,
})
export class App {
  protected readonly enabled = signal(true);
  protected readonly value = signal(1);
  protected readonly multiplier = multiplier;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Cheers!</p>

Q: How do you find the key and value at a certain position in a C++ map? A: <p>It seems you don't actually want to get key/value at a specific position, but rather loop over the map. Then:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>for (const auto &amp;elem : m)
{
    // Here `elem.first` is the key and `elem.second` is the value.
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you also need to know the index, then compute it yourself by defining <code>int i = 0;</code> and incrementing it on each iteration.</p>
<p>If you really wanted to get key/value at a specific position, you'd do <code>std::next(m.begin(), i)-&gt;first</code> for key and <code>...-&gt;second</code> for value, but this is slower than looping, because it requires traversing the map from the beginning to <code>i</code> every time, while the loop would only traverse it once.</p>

Q: cURL request error 400 trying to access langSearch API A: <p>First, you encode data but send not encoded data as array:</p>
<pre><code>$payload = json_encode($params);
curl_setopt($curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $params); // use $payload here
</code></pre>
<p>The second one is headers:</p>
<pre><code>$headers = [
    &quot;Authorisation: Bearer &quot; . $LANGSEARCH_KEY,
    &quot;Content Type: application/json&quot;
];
</code></pre>
<p>Typo in word <code>Authorisation</code>  - use Z not s - <code>Authorization</code> , and check <code>Content Type</code> write correct or not</p>
